#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Lists each open line of an issue that asks for evidence in the PR, and
with `--check BODY.md` names each one the PR body gives no evidence for.

Usage: plan/bin/pr-evidence <issue> [--check BODY.md [--head SHA] [--pr N] [--flake-ticket] [--closes N]...]
       plan/bin/pr-evidence --check BODY.md [--head SHA] [--closes N]...    (a body with no ticket, such as a retro's)

A line asks for evidence in the PR when it says so: "Evidence is ... in the
PR", "The PR lists", "Record the size in the PR", "Attach" (but not
"attached to this issue"), "Link the run", "PR description". Its key is its
first sentence, or its first two when another line starts the same way. The
body gives each key a list item, `- <key>: <evidence>` (the key's closing
stop may go), or a list item under
`## Still open` when this PR leaves it for later. Evidence that is empty or
starts "Not done" or "TODO" counts as none. A ticked box is left out, since
an earlier PR gave its evidence.

#61's body draft left out the reviewer's checklist its timer-guide box asked
for, though the checklist was drafted, until the lead found it (#61 retro).

`--check` also refuses an unfilled placeholder outside inline code: a
capitalised token joined by -, such as LOOP-OUTPUT, that ends in a word of
SUFFIXES. #399's draft held LOOP-OUTPUT where its text said "all 25 pass",
and this check passed it (#399 retro). A token joined by _ of that shape is
one too unless a file under server/ or testing/ assigns it (`NAME = ...`,
`NAME=...`, an enum member), since code names such as VIEW_RACE_RESULTS share
the shape: #406's draft held HANDOFF_RUNS, as #399's had, and review found it
(#406 retro). A mention in prose, a README, a comment, a string or plan/ does
not excuse a token, since #406's review found LOG_TABLE, EXP_TABLE and
NO_MORE_RESULTS passing on a vendored script and a timer profile, and a plan
README row once passed HANDOFF_RUNS. The limit left: an assignment of the same
name anywhere under server/ or testing/ excuses a placeholder written to look
like it, and a name PHP, Java or JavaScript defines is refused until it is put
in inline code.

`--check` also refuses a closing keyword outside code that names an issue
other than <issue> and each `--closes N` (#404 closed #65 and #64): GitHub's
close, closes, closed, fix, fixes, fixed, resolve, resolves or resolved,
with any colons, blanks or - between, before #N, GH-N, owner/repo#N or an
issue URL. GitHub closes that issue on merge. `--closes` takes N or #N, a
positive number, and refuses anything else. A reference into a repo other
than PorkChopExpress86/derbynet (`$REPO`), by owner/repo#N or by URL, is
refused whatever its number. GH-N is an autolinked reference form in GitHub's
docs ("Autolinked references and URLs"); that closing keywords honour it is
unverified, so it counts, and a PR that needs one quotes it in backticks.
#421's body quoted a test fixture's
"Closes #399." in prose, and pr-merge refused it at merge time only because
#399 had a `## Still open` section; #411's retro body was the first (#415
retro). Quoted text goes in a code span (a run of backticks and the next run
of as many, so ``Closes #1`` is one) or a closed code fence, which GitHub
does not read; a fence opens on a line of three or more backticks with no
backtick after them, and closes on a bare line of as many or more. The lines
of a fence that never closes are checked, as pr-merge reads cited decisions.
A code span breaks a keyword it sits in or between ("Clo`x`ses #1", "Closes
`x` #1" close nothing). The limits left: a keyword before a PR's number is
refused too, though GitHub closes only issues; a code span that runs over a
line end is not read as one.

Given <issue>, `--check` also refuses a body that names it in no closing
keyword (as above, into this repo) and no "Part of #<issue>", outside code.
#444's body named #434 only in prose, so its merge left #434 open, and the
lead closed it by hand (#434 retro). A ticket the PR leaves open writes
"Part of #N" beside its `## Still open` section, which pr-merge asks of it.
Here "outside code" is stricter than for the check above: it leaves out
whatever GitHub may draw as code or hide, so an HTML comment, <pre> and
<code>, a ~~~ fence, a fence that never closes, a quoted fence and an
indented line hold no link (`unquoted_lines(strict=True)`; the limit left and
GitHub's rendering are in its docstring).

A list item `- CI load: <run URL>` cites a run of
.github/workflows/ci-load.yml as load evidence (#415). `--check` reads the
run from GitHub and accepts it only when it ran master's workflow
(workflow_dispatch, head_branch master: `--ref <branch>` runs that branch's
copy), on its first attempt (a "Re-run failed jobs" turns a failed run green),
at the PR's head with no trial input, and passed. The run's name is exactly
`CI load at <SHA>`, or `CI load at <SHA> (flake ticket)` for a run with
`-f flake_ticket=true`; the workflow names a run with a `base`, `files`,
`count`, `loops_per_cpu` or `workers` input `CI load trial ...`. A flake
ticket's whole-file 25 with `--flake-ticket` is the evidence D101 and D122
ask of it, so with `--flake-ticket` the run must carry the `(flake ticket)`
name, and a plain run is refused. The ticket's body and title do not say
reliably that it is a flake ticket (#373 and #407 both name the flag), so
the caller says it (plan/implement-all-tickets.md, step 3.2). The head is
`--head SHA`, or PR N's head as GitHub reads it with `--pr N`; given both,
they must match. It is labelled CI load: extra evidence until the owner
amends D85, never a local load run. Other links to runs in the body are not
read.

`--check` also refuses a hand-off header that names a head other than the
PR's (#427). A hand-off log starts with the line the brief asks for, which the
body keeps when it pastes the output: `cwd <dir> HEAD <sha>`, `# HEAD <sha>`,
`HEAD: <sha>`, `cwd=<dir> head=<sha>`, `head <sha>` (the line tools/detach.sh
prints), `worktree <dir> head <sha>`. A header is a line that starts with
`cwd`, `worktree`, `HEAD` or `head` (after any `#`, `>` or `$`), and for
`cwd` and `worktree` names the head after the directory. Its sha, 7 to 40 hex
digits, must be a prefix of `--head SHA` (or of PR N's head, with `--pr N`),
else the body names the line: the run is older than the last edit
(#407's retro mutants ran two commits before its final head, #413's first
mutation round at an older head). A line the body means to leave at another
head carries `[before run]` (BEFORE_RUN) on the header line itself or on the
nearest line above it, outside the fence the header sits in: a reproduction
on the base, a control run on master, the run before the fix. The marker is
the author's word that the run is meant to be older, and the limit left:
nothing checks that it is. D113 lets a branch skip the rerun when master's new
commits touch nothing it reads, so a header that names the commit before a
merge is accepted too: when `--head` is a merge commit, each commit on its
first-parent line down to the first commit that is no merge passes
(`merge_chain`), read from the checkout's git; nothing checks what the merged
commits touched, which `plan/bin/pr-merge` does. A body with a header and no
`--head` or `--pr` is refused for it, as a `CI load:` item is. Only a header
line is read: prose that names an older head, such as a round's history, a
mutant's note or a `load run:` line that names another run's head, is not. The
sweep over the 285 bodies merged by 2026-11-20 is in the PR of #427.

Exits 1 on a key the body gives no evidence for, on a CI load item not
accepted or given with no head, on a hand-off header that is not at the PR's
head and not marked, or given with no head, on a `--head` that is not PR N's
head, on a placeholder, on a closing keyword for another issue, on a body that
names <issue> in no closing keyword and no "Part of", or when the issue, the
PR or a run cannot be read.
"""

import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys

ASKS = re.compile(
    r"\b(?:in|to) the PR\b|\bthe PR (?:lists|names|says|records|shows|explains)\b"
    r"|\bPR (?:description|body)\b|\battach|\blink the run\b",
    re.IGNORECASE,
)
ELSEWHERE = re.compile(r"\b(?:to|on|in) this issue\b", re.IGNORECASE)
ITEM = re.compile(r"^\s*[-*] (?:\[( |x|X)\] )?(.*)$")
SENTENCES = re.compile(r".*?[.!?](?=\s|$)")
NO_EVIDENCE = re.compile(r"^(?:not done|todo)\b", re.IGNORECASE)
# A draft's stand-in for output still to come, such as PROBE-PENDING or
# LOOP-OUTPUT in #399's drafts. A shell or Python name has no -. A path
# (path/X-OUTPUT, X-OUTPUT.txt) or a shell default (${X-OUTPUT}) passes.
SUFFIXES = ("PENDING", "RESULT", "RESULTS", "OUTPUT", "RUNS", "TABLE", "TODO", "TBD")
PLACEHOLDER = re.compile(
    rf"(?<![A-Za-z0-9$/.-])(?<!\$\{{)[A-Z][A-Z0-9]*(?:-[A-Z0-9]+)*-(?:{'|'.join(SUFFIXES)})"
    r"(?![A-Za-z0-9/-]|\.\w)"
)
# The same shape joined by _, as #399's and #406's drafts wrote
# HANDOFF_RUNS. It counts only when no file under CODE_DIRS assigns it
# (defined_names).
JOINED = re.compile(
    rf"(?<![A-Za-z0-9$/._-])(?<!\$\{{)[A-Z][A-Z0-9]*(?:_[A-Z0-9]+)*_(?:{'|'.join(SUFFIXES)})"
    r"(?![A-Za-z0-9_/-]|\.\w)"
)
INLINE_CODE = re.compile(r"`[^`\n]*`")
# CommonMark's code span: a run of backticks, then the next run of exactly as
# many. A run with no such partner is literal text. Used by unquoted_lines;
# `placeholders` still reads INLINE_CODE, which takes ``X`` as two empty spans.
CODE_SPAN = re.compile(r"(?<!`)(`+)(?!`).+?(?<!`)\1(?!`)")
# A code fence's line: three or more backticks, then an info string with no
# backtick (CommonMark). "```code``` here" is inline code, not a fence.
FENCE = re.compile(r"^\s*(`{3,})([^`]*)$")
# The same for a ~~~ fence, which `unquoted_lines` reads only strictly, an
# indented code block's line (four spaces or a tab), a blockquote's marker,
# and the HTML that GitHub's renderer draws as code or not at all: a comment,
# <pre> and <code>, each with the tag that ends it.
TILDE_FENCE = re.compile(r"^\s*(~{3,})(.*)$")
INDENTED = re.compile(r"^(?: {4}|\t)")
QUOTE = re.compile(r"^\s{0,3}(?:>\s?)+")
RAW_OPEN = re.compile(r"<!--|<(pre|code)\b", re.IGNORECASE)
RAW_CLOSE = {"<!--": "-->", "<pre": "</pre>", "<code": "</code>"}
# GitHub's closing keywords before an issue reference (#N, GH-N,
# owner/repo#N, or an issue URL). Checked against every PR body on the fork:
# the issues it finds are GitHub's closingIssuesReferences but for one PR
# number (#377's "closed #325").
# What lies between the keyword and the reference is read wide (any run of
# colons, blanks and -, none at all, a letter after the number), since a
# miss closes an issue and a false refusal costs a pair of backticks.
# GH-N is one of GitHub's autolinked reference forms
# (docs.github.com, "Autolinked references and URLs": "GH-26"); that its
# closing keywords honour it is unverified, so it is read as closing.
CLOSING = re.compile(
    r"(?<!\w)(?:close[sd]?|fix(?:e[sd])?|resolve[sd]?)[:\s-]*"
    r"(?:(?:(?P<repo>[\w.-]+/[\w.-]+)#|#|GH-)(?P<number>\d+)"
    r"|https://github\.com/(?P<url_repo>[\w.-]+/[\w.-]+)/issues/(?P<url_number>\d+))",
    re.IGNORECASE,
)
# The line a hand-off log starts with, as the fork's merged bodies and saved
# logs write it (`cwd X HEAD S`, `cwd: X  HEAD: S`, `cwd=X head=S`, `# HEAD S`,
# `HEAD: S`, `HEAD S`, `worktree X head S`, ...); tools/detach.sh prints
# `head S`. It starts with a keyword, so prose and a `load run:` line that
# name a head are not read. The tests' HEADER_SHAPES lists each shape.
HEADER = re.compile(
    r"^\s*(?:[#>$]\s*)*(?:(?:cwd|worktree)\b.*?\b)?head\b[:=]?\s*(?P<sha>[0-9a-f]{7,40})\b",
    re.IGNORECASE,
)
BEFORE_RUN = "[before run]"
CI_LOAD_ITEM = re.compile(r"^CI load:\s*(.*)$", re.IGNORECASE)
RUN_URL = re.compile(r"https://github\.com/([\w.-]+/[\w.-]+)/actions/runs/(\d+)")
CI_LOAD_WORKFLOW = ".github/workflows/ci-load.yml"
ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))))
# Where a code name is assigned. plan/ is left out: it holds this tool, its
# test and the README rows and notes that quote the drafts' placeholders.
CODE_DIRS = ("server", "testing")


def fold(text: str) -> str:
    """Text with runs of white space folded and case dropped."""
    return " ".join(text.split()).casefold()


def items(body: str) -> list[tuple[str | None, str]]:
    """Each list item as (its box mark or None, its text), with the indented
    lines that continue it joined on."""
    found: list[tuple[str | None, str]] = []
    open_item = False
    for line in body.splitlines():
        item = ITEM.match(line)
        if item:
            found.append((item[1], item[2].strip()))
            open_item = True
        elif open_item and line[:1].isspace() and line.strip():
            mark, text = found[-1]
            found[-1] = (mark, f"{text} {line.strip()}")
        else:
            open_item = False
    return found


def keys(body: str) -> list[str]:
    """The key of each open list item that asks for evidence in the PR, in
    the issue's order, each once."""
    asking = []
    for mark, text in items(body):
        if mark in ("x", "X") or not ASKS.search(text) or ELSEWHERE.search(text):
            continue
        asking.append([s.strip() for s in SENTENCES.findall(text)] or [text])
    found = []
    for sentences in asking:
        key = sentences[0]
        alike = {tuple(other) for other in asking if other[0] == key}
        if len(alike) > 1 and len(sentences) > 1:
            key = f"{key} {sentences[1]}"
        if key not in found:
            found.append(key)
    return found


def body_items(body: str) -> tuple[list[str], list[str]]:
    """The folded list items of a PR body: those outside `## Still open`,
    and those under it."""
    outside: list[str] = []
    still_open: list[str] = []
    section = None
    chunk: list[str] = []
    for line in [*body.splitlines(), "## "]:
        if line.startswith("## "):
            target = still_open if section == "Still open" else outside
            target.extend(fold(text) for _mark, text in items("\n".join(chunk)))
            section, chunk = line[3:].strip(), []
        else:
            chunk.append(line)
    return outside, still_open


def has_evidence(key: str, outside: list[str], still_open: list[str]) -> bool:
    """Whether an item reads `<key>: <evidence>` with evidence that is not
    "Not done" or "TODO", or an item under `## Still open` starts with the
    key. The key's closing stop may be left out before the colon."""
    k = fold(key)
    for stem in {k, k.rstrip(".!?")}:
        for item in outside:
            if item.startswith(stem + ":"):
                rest = item[len(stem) + 1 :].strip()
                if rest and not NO_EVIDENCE.match(rest):
                    return True
    return any(item.startswith(k.rstrip(".!?")) for item in still_open)


def defined_names(tokens: set[str]) -> set[str]:
    """The tokens a file under CODE_DIRS of this checkout assigns: a line
    that starts `NAME = x`, `NAME=x` or `export NAME=x`, with no second `=`
    after it. Git runs without the GIT_* variables a hook sets."""
    if not tokens:
        return set()
    env = {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if not k.startswith("GIT_")}
    names = "|".join(sorted(tokens))
    pattern = f"^[[:blank:]]*((export|readonly)[[:blank:]]+)?({names})[[:blank:]]*=([^=]|$)"
    done = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "-C", ROOT, "grep", "-ohE", pattern, "--", *CODE_DIRS],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        env=env,
    )
    if done.returncode not in (0, 1):
        sys.exit(f"pr-evidence: git grep could not read the tree:\n{done.stderr.strip()}")
    return {line.split("=")[0].split()[-1] for line in done.stdout.splitlines()}


def placeholders(body: str, defined=defined_names) -> list[str]:
    """Each unfilled placeholder, as `line N: TOKEN`: a token joined by -,
    or one joined by _ that `defined` does not find assigned in the tree."""
    found = []
    for n, line in enumerate(body.splitlines(), 1):
        bare = INLINE_CODE.sub("", line)
        found += [(n, m.start(), m[0]) for m in PLACEHOLDER.finditer(bare)]
        found += [(n, m.start(), m[0]) for m in JOINED.finditer(bare)]
    code_names = defined({token for _n, _at, token in found if "_" in token})
    return [f"line {n}: {token}" for n, _at, token in sorted(found) if token not in code_names]


def unquoted_lines(body: str, strict: bool = False) -> list[tuple[int, str]]:
    """Each line GitHub reads as prose, as (its number, the line with each
    code span replaced by a NUL, which breaks a keyword or reference it sits
    in or between): a closed code fence is left out, and the lines of a fence
    that never closes are kept, as pr-merge reads cited decisions. Only a
    bare line of at least as many backticks as the opener closes a fence.

    `strict` also leaves out whatever GitHub may read as code or hide: the
    lines of a fence that never closes, a ~~~ fence, an HTML comment, <pre>
    and <code> (each to its closing tag, or to the end), and a line indented
    four spaces or a tab, after a blockquote's `>` markers are taken off. A
    lazy continuation line, a nested list item and an unclosed comment that
    starts mid-line are left out too, though GitHub links in them: a false
    refusal costs a move of the line, a missed one costs an open ticket. A
    refusal of a close reads the default, since it must see all GitHub may
    act on; a check that a link is there reads `strict`, since it must accept
    only what GitHub surely reads. The limit left: other raw HTML, such as a
    table cell or a reference-link definition, is read as prose. `gh api
    markdown` drew "Part of #61" as code or not at all in each form the
    strict reading leaves out, and linked it in each it keeps (the #434
    retro's PR body pastes the runs); that GitHub's closing keywords follow
    the renderer is assumed, since they act on a merge and cannot be read
    without one."""
    kept, fence, raw_end = [], None, None
    for n, line in enumerate(body.splitlines(), 1):
        if strict:
            line = QUOTE.sub("", line)
        if raw_end:
            at = line.lower().find(raw_end)
            if at < 0:
                continue
            raw_end, line = None, line[at + len(raw_end):]
        edge = FENCE.match(line)
        tilde = TILDE_FENCE.match(line) if strict else None
        if fence is None:
            if edge:
                fence = (len(edge[1]), [], "`")
            elif tilde:
                fence = (len(tilde[1]), [], "~")
            elif strict and INDENTED.match(line):
                continue
            else:
                text = CODE_SPAN.sub("\0", line)
                while strict and (opener := RAW_OPEN.search(text)):
                    end = RAW_CLOSE[opener[0].lower()]
                    at = text.lower().find(end, opener.end())
                    if at < 0:
                        text, raw_end = text[: opener.start()], end
                    else:
                        text = text[: opener.start()] + "\0" + text[at + len(end):]
                kept.append((n, text))
        elif fence[2] == "`" and edge and not edge[2].strip() and len(edge[1]) >= fence[0]:
            fence = None
        elif fence[2] == "~" and tilde and not tilde[2].strip() and len(tilde[1]) >= fence[0]:
            fence = None
        else:
            fence[1].append((n, line))
    if fence and not strict:
        kept += [(n, CODE_SPAN.sub("\0", line)) for n, line in fence[1]]
    return sorted(kept)


def closing_refs(body: str, strict: bool = False) -> list[tuple[int, str, str, bool]]:
    """Each closing keyword outside code, as (its line number, the words, the
    issue number, whether the reference is into this repo, the fork)."""
    fork = os.environ.get("REPO", "PorkChopExpress86/derbynet").casefold()
    found = []
    for n, line in unquoted_lines(body, strict):
        for m in CLOSING.finditer(line):
            repo = m["repo"] or m["url_repo"]
            found.append((n, m[0], m["number"] or m["url_number"],
                          not repo or repo.casefold() == fork))
    return found


def foreign_closes(body: str, closes: set[str]) -> list[str]:
    """Each closing keyword outside code for an issue not in `closes`, or in
    a repo other than the fork, as `line N: <the words>`."""
    return [f"line {n}: {words}" for n, words, number, in_fork in closing_refs(body)
            if number not in closes or not in_fork]


def links_ticket(body: str, issue: str) -> bool:
    """Whether a line GitHub surely reads as prose (`unquoted_lines` strict)
    closes `issue` in this repo or says "Part of #<issue>"."""
    part_of = re.compile(rf"(?<!\w)part of #{issue}(?!\w)", re.IGNORECASE)
    return (any(part_of.search(line) for _n, line in unquoted_lines(body, strict=True))
            or any(number == issue and in_fork
                   for _n, _words, number, in_fork in closing_refs(body, strict=True)))


def handoff_headers(body: str) -> list[tuple[int, str, str, bool]]:
    """Each hand-off header as (its line number, the line, its sha in lower
    case, whether it is marked `[before run]`): on the line, or on the nearest
    non-blank line above it. For a header inside a code fence that is the
    line above the fence's opener, so one caption marks the fence's headers."""
    lines = body.splitlines()
    found, fence = [], None
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        edge = FENCE.match(line)
        if fence is None and edge:
            fence = (len(edge[1]), i)
            continue
        if fence and edge and not edge[2].strip() and len(edge[1]) >= fence[0]:
            fence = None
            continue
        header = HEADER.match(line)
        if header:
            caption = next((above for above in reversed(lines[:fence[1] if fence else i])
                            if above.strip()), "")
            marked = BEFORE_RUN.casefold() in f"{line} {caption}".casefold()
            found.append((i + 1, line.strip(), header["sha"].casefold(), marked))
    return found


def merge_chain(head: str, root: str = ROOT) -> list[str]:
    """The commits D113 lets a header name for a `head` that is a merge: its
    first parent while the commit is a merge, down to the first that is none,
    which is the branch's own last commit. Empty when git cannot read `head`,
    and for a commit that is no merge."""
    env = {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if not k.startswith("GIT_")}
    chain, commit = [], head
    for _ in range(100):
        done = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "rev-list", "--parents", "-n", "1", commit],
                              capture_output=True, text=True, env=env)
        parents = done.stdout.split()[1:]
        if len(parents) < 2:
            break
        commit = parents[0]
        chain.append(commit)
    return chain


def stale_headers(body: str, head: str | None, chain=merge_chain) -> list[str]:
    """A line for each unmarked hand-off header: `line N: <the line>` with
    why it is refused, when its sha is no prefix of `head` and names none of
    the commits before a merge at `head` (`chain`), or when there is no head."""
    unmarked = [h for h in handoff_headers(body) if not h[3]]
    if not head:
        return [f"line {n}: {line}: no --head or --pr to check it at"
                for n, line, _sha, _marked in unmarked]
    head = head.casefold()
    stale = [h for h in unmarked if not head.startswith(h[2])]
    before = [c.casefold() for c in chain(head)] if stale else []
    return [f"line {n}: {line}: the PR's head is {head}, so this run is older than its last "
            f"edit; run it again at the head, or write {BEFORE_RUN} on it if it is a before, "
            "reproduction or control run"
            for n, line, sha, _m in stale if not any(c.startswith(sha) for c in before)]


def ci_load_items(body: str) -> list[str]:
    """The evidence text of each `CI load:` item outside `## Still open`."""
    found, section, chunk = [], None, []
    for line in [*body.splitlines(), "## "]:
        if line.startswith("## "):
            if section != "Still open":
                texts = (" ".join(text.split()) for _mark, text in items("\n".join(chunk)))
                found += [m[1] for m in map(CI_LOAD_ITEM.match, texts) if m]
            section, chunk = line[3:].strip(), []
        else:
            chunk.append(line)
    return found


def ci_load_verdict(run: dict, head: str, flake_ticket: bool = False) -> str | None:
    """None when a run, as GitHub's runs API answers, is a passed first
    attempt of master's CI load workflow at head with no trial input, else
    why not. For a flake ticket the run must be the `(flake ticket)` one."""
    if run.get("path") != CI_LOAD_WORKFLOW:
        return f"not a run of {CI_LOAD_WORKFLOW} but of {run.get('path')}"
    if run.get("event") != "workflow_dispatch":
        return f"started by {run.get('event')}, not workflow_dispatch"
    if run.get("head_branch") != "master":
        return f"ran the workflow of {run.get('head_branch')}, not master's"
    if run.get("run_attempt") != 1:
        return f"attempt {run.get('run_attempt')}, not the first"
    title = run.get("display_title")
    if title not in (f"CI load at {head}", f"CI load at {head} (flake ticket)"):
        return f"{title!r} is not at the PR's head {head}"
    if flake_ticket and title != f"CI load at {head} (flake ticket)":
        return (f"{title!r} ran without flake_ticket, and this ticket is a flake ticket: "
                "D101 asks its whole-file 25 with --flake-ticket (D122)")
    if (run.get("status"), run.get("conclusion")) != ("completed", "success"):
        return f"{run.get('status')}, {run.get('conclusion')}: not passed"
    return None


def check_ci_load(body: str, head: str | None, flake_ticket: bool = False) -> list[str]:
    """A line for each `CI load:` item: accepted, or why not (the line then
    starts "not load evidence")."""
    lines = []
    for text in ci_load_items(body):
        url = RUN_URL.search(text)
        if not url:
            lines.append(f"not load evidence, no run URL: CI load: {text}")
            continue
        if not head:
            lines.append(f"not load evidence, no --head or --pr to check it at: {url[0]}")
            continue
        repo = os.environ.get("REPO", "PorkChopExpress86/derbynet")
        if url[1].casefold() != repo.casefold():
            lines.append(f"not load evidence, a run of {url[1]}, not {repo}: {url[0]}")
            continue
        run = gh_json(["api", f"repos/{repo}/actions/runs/{url[2]}"], url[0])
        why = ci_load_verdict(run, head, flake_ticket)
        if why:
            lines.append(f"not load evidence, {why}: {url[0]}")
        else:
            lines.append(f"{run['display_title']}, passed: {url[0]} (extra evidence until "
                         "the owner amends D85; it replaces no local load run)")
    return lines


def gh_json(args: list[str], what: str) -> dict:
    gh = os.environ.get("GH", "gh")
    done = subprocess.run([gh, *args], capture_output=True, text=True)
    if done.returncode:
        sys.exit(f"pr-evidence: gh could not read {what}:\n{done.stderr.strip()}")
    return json.loads(done.stdout)


def issue_body(number: str) -> str:
    gh = os.environ.get("GH", "gh")
    repo = os.environ.get("REPO", "PorkChopExpress86/derbynet")
    done = subprocess.run(
        [gh, "issue", "view", number, "--repo", repo, "--json", "body", "--jq", ".body"],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
    )
    if done.returncode:
        sys.exit(f"pr-evidence: gh could not read #{number}:\n{done.stderr.strip()}")
    return done.stdout.replace("\r", "")


def issue_number(text: str) -> str:
    """An issue number as --closes takes it: N or #N, positive, no zeros in front."""
    found = re.fullmatch(r"#?([1-9]\d*)", text)
    if not found:
        raise argparse.ArgumentTypeError(
            f"takes an issue number such as 64 or #64, not {text!r}")
    return found[1]


class Parser(argparse.ArgumentParser):
    def error(self, message):
        sys.exit(f"pr-evidence: {message}\n\n{__doc__}")


def pr_head(number: str) -> str:
    repo = os.environ.get("REPO", "PorkChopExpress86/derbynet")
    return gh_json(["pr", "view", number, "--repo", repo, "--json", "headRefOid"],
                   f"PR #{number}")["headRefOid"]


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    parser = Parser(prog="plan/bin/pr-evidence", allow_abbrev=False, add_help=False)
    parser.add_argument("issue", nargs="?")
    parser.add_argument("--check", metavar="BODY.md")
    parser.add_argument("--head", metavar="SHA")
    parser.add_argument("--pr", metavar="N")
    parser.add_argument("--flake-ticket", action="store_true")
    parser.add_argument("--closes", metavar="N", type=issue_number, action="append", default=[])
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    if (args.head or args.pr or args.flake_ticket or args.closes) and not args.check:
        parser.error("--head, --pr, --flake-ticket and --closes go with --check")
    if not (args.issue or args.check):
        parser.error("give an issue, or --check BODY.md")
    head = args.head
    if args.pr:
        real = pr_head(args.pr)
        if head and head != real:
            sys.exit(f"pr-evidence: --head {head} is not the head of PR #{args.pr}, {real}")
        head = real
    asked = keys(issue_body(args.issue)) if args.issue else []
    if not args.check:
        for key in asked:
            print(f"- {key.rstrip('.!?')}: ")
        print(f"{len(asked)} open lines of #{args.issue} ask for evidence in the PR")
        return 0
    with open(args.check, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        body = f.read()
    outside, still_open = body_items(body)
    missing = [key for key in asked if not has_evidence(key, outside, still_open)]
    for key in missing:
        print(f"no evidence in the body: {key}")
    if args.issue:
        print(
            f"{len(asked) - len(missing)} of {len(asked)} open lines of #{args.issue} that ask "
            "for evidence in the PR have it in the body"
        )
    else:
        print("no issue given: no ticket's evidence lines were checked")
    unfilled = placeholders(body)
    for found in unfilled:
        print(f"unfilled placeholder, {found}")
    closes = foreign_closes(body, {args.issue, *args.closes} - {None})
    for found in closes:
        print(f"closes another issue, {found}: GitHub closes it on merge; put a quote in "
              "backticks, or write \"Part of #N\"")
    unlinked = bool(args.issue) and not links_ticket(body, args.issue)
    if unlinked:
        print(f"no \"Closes #{args.issue}\" or \"Part of #{args.issue}\" outside code: the "
              f"merge leaves #{args.issue} open with no link to the PR (an HTML comment, "
              "<pre>, <code>, a ~~~ fence and an indented line count as code)")
    stale = stale_headers(body, head)
    for line in stale:
        print(f"hand-off head not the PR's, {line}")
    loads = check_ci_load(body, head, args.flake_ticket)
    for line in loads:
        print(line)
    refused = [line for line in loads if line.startswith("not load evidence")]
    return 1 if missing or unfilled or closes or unlinked or stale or refused else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
